CloudBurrow patch 107 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#2120). Applies after patch 0106.

BigQuery: a table function's parameter may be a table, TABLE<column
type, ...>, which "accepts an input table containing a superset of
those columns in any order", or ANY TABLE, a table of any columns; a call
gives it as TABLE dataset.table or a subquery
(https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/table-functions#table_parameters,
and the CREATE TABLE FUNCTION statement of the data definition language
reference). The engine panicked on CREATE TABLE FUNCTION of a TABLE<...>
parameter, and refused one of ANY TABLE (or ANY TYPE) ("TVF body is
missing").

A TABLE<...> parameter is now kept in the function's spec with its
columns, and registered as a relation argument of that schema, extra
columns allowed; the analyzer matches a call's table to it by column
name and coerces each column, and refuses a column of a type that does
not coerce ("Invalid type STRING for column "year INT64" of argument 1
of ...") or one missing ("Required column "year" not found in table
passed as argument 1 of ..."). The body's reads of the parameter
(ResolvedRelationArgumentScan) are formatted as a subquery of @name, and
the call's table, a subquery of the parameter's columns, put in its
place. A function with an ANY TABLE or ANY TYPE parameter is registered
as a templated SQL table function, whose body the analyzer resolves at
each call; the formatter analyzes it again for the call's own argument
types to format it. Arguments are put in their @name's place in one
pass, so a parameter named t is no longer put into @t1. Regression test
TestTableFunctionTableParameters.

